i literally always have pho broth in my  freezer and i'll just sit there and  drink it out of a mug  hi my name is susan i'm a recipe  developer and i'm going to show you how  to make vietnamese fur  [Applause]  sofa is a traditional vietnamese style  noodle soup it's just the name for  actually the noodles pho means rice  noodles one of the most important  aspects of the soup is the stock to make  a really good stock you have to start  with good bones we're going to do  something called blanching your bones it  helps to take out a lot of the  impurities that you end up skimming  later my parents were both born in  vietnam and i was born here in america  and when we came here my dad was all  about becoming americans but my mom  would still cook us vietnamese food  because she felt it was important for us  to always know from our culture she  would always make these big feasts that  would revolve around something like a  fur just a big pot that goes up at the  beginning of the day then we would have  my cousins over our neighbors when my  mother first took me to vietnam and  living in america we always had fun at  lunch or dinner i remember waking up in  the morning my mom was like let's go to  breakfast and i'm sitting in ho chi minh  city nearly on the ground as like an 11  year old child eating pho first thing in  the morning it's the hottest most  delicious thing i've ever eaten and i  remember we never knew the name of the  person that made this bowl of pho we  literally just called her the old noodle  lady and my sister and i still talk  about it like to this day like do you  remember those old lady needles because  they were so good my parents had a store  in downtown seattle and right next door  to it there was a place called a little  bit of saigon i was always super bored  at my parents store so i would go next  door to the restaurant and i became  friends with one of the servers and she  kind of let me make the desserts  sometimes or make drinks and deliver  them to tables that was my playground my  mother ended up going back to vietnam  when i was in third grade so when i  wanted to have a little piece of home  i would have to learn how to cook for  myself i love making food at home  because i think that making soups of any  kind especially ones are a little bit  labor intensive it's as good as you want  to make it i take all these extra steps  to make sure at the end of eight hours  it's worth all that effort i really love  that i can have some for now and then  i'll have some for later i literally  always have pho broth in my freezer when  i moved from seattle which had a really  large vietnamese population i go next  door to a little bit of saigon and have  the best bowl of flavor when i came to  new york not quite as large of a  population of vietnamese i actually  started cooking it more when i moved to  new york because i was like if i want a  good bowl of i gotta cook it for myself  in the vietnamese culture we place a ton  of importance on food and how it can  bring people together i remember being  younger in seattle thinking that i was a  little weird because i went to a school  and most of my friends had like peanut  butter and jelly sandwiches and like my  mom didn't know what peanut butter and  jelly was so she would just pack me a  bun me and i thought it was like the  most delicious thing ever and my  friend's like what the heck is pate and  i'd be like it's so good now 2018  bunnies are sold on every corner it's  really amazing to me how far vietnamese  cuisine has come and i really love that  it's getting a little bit more  recognition it's really cool to see  other cultures come to appreciate it and  honestly makes it a little bit easier  for me to find good food that reminds me  of home food is memories and it's  important and i can have a bun me and a  fa and it brings me back to like eating  fun at stoop on ho chi minh city  i just want to eat the broth the noodles  are delicious but i just want to sip the  broth all day  you
 

